response:
 First, we calculate the future value of the investment with a simple interest rate of \(6\%\). The formula for simple interest is \(A = P (1 + rt)\), where \(P\) is the principal amount, \(r\) is the annual interest rate, and \(t\) is the time in years. Here, \(P = 20,\!000\), \(r = 0.06\), and \(t = 4\). So the future value with a simple interest rate of \(6\%\) is: \[
A_{\text{simple}} = 20,\!000 \left(1 + 0.06 \cdot 4\right) = 20,\!000 \left(1 + 0.24\right) = 20,\!000 \cdot 1.24 = 24,\!800 \text{ dollars}
\]

Next, we calculate the future value of the investment with a compound interest rate of \(7\%\) that compounds quarterly. The formula for compound interest is \(A = P \left(1 + \frac{r}{n}\right)^{nt}\), where \(n\) is the number of times interest is compounded per year. Here, \(P = 20,\!000\), \(r = 0.07\), \(n = 4\) (since it compounds quarterly), and \(t = 4\). So the future value with a compound interest rate of \(7\%\) is: \[
A_{\text{compound}} = 20,\!000 \left(1 + \frac{0.07}{4}\right)^{4 \cdot 4} = 20,\!000 \left(1 + 0.0175\right)^{16} \approx 20,\!000 \cdot 1.3199 \approx 26,\!398 \text{ dollars}
\]

The difference between the two future values is: \[
26,\!398 - 24,\!800 = 1,\!598 \text{ dollars}
\]

Rounded to the nearest dollar, the difference is \(1,\!598\) dollars. So the final answer is: \[
\boxed{1598}
\]
